1
00:00:02,421 --> 00:00:07,979
January, began to heal and subside under the gentler breathings of April.

2
00:00:08,959 --> 00:00:12,202
The nights and mornings no longer by their Canadian

3
00:00:12,203 --> 00:00:15,600
temperature, froze the very blood in our veins.

4
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:19,959
We could now endure the play hour passed in the garden.

5
00:00:20,950 --> 00:00:27,399
Sometimes on a sunny day, it began even to be pleasant and genial, and a greenness

6
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:33,360
grew over those brown beds, which, freshening daily, suggested the thought

7
00:00:33,361 --> 00:00:39,960
that hope traversed them at night, and left each morning brighter traces of her steps.

8
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:45,540
Flowers peeped out amongst the leaves, snow drops, crocuses, purple auriculas,

9
00:00:46,219 --> 00:00:47,940
and golden-eyed pansies.

10
00:00:49,379 --> 00:00:55,840
On Thursday afternoon's half holidays, we now took walks and found still sweeter

11
00:00:55,841 --> 00:00:58,420
flowers opening by the wayside under the hedges.

12
00:00:59,549 --> 00:01:05,979
I discovered too that a great pleasure and enjoyment which the horizon only bounded

13
00:01:06,629 --> 00:01:10,899
lay all outside the high and spike-guarded walls of our garden.

14
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:17,340
This pleasure consisted in prospect of noble summits, girdling a great hill

15
00:01:17,341 --> 00:01:21,864
hollow, rich in verger and shadow, in a bright

16
00:01:21,865 --> 00:01:25,559
beck full of dark stones and sparkling eddies.

17
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:31,920
How different had this scene looked when I viewed it laid out beneath the iron sky of

18
00:01:31,921 --> 00:01:35,620
winter, stiffened in frost, shrouded with snow.

19
00:01:36,629 --> 00:01:41,319
When mists as chill as death, wandered to the impulse of east winds along those

20
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:45,361
purple peaks, and rolled down, eng, and home till

21
00:01:45,362 --> 00:01:48,819
they blended with the frozen fog of the beck.

22
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:53,059
That beck itself was then a torrent, turbid and curbless.

23
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:57,620
It tore a sun to the wood and sent a raving sound through the air, often

24
00:01:57,620 --> 00:01:59,899
thickened with wild rain or whirling sleet.

25
00:02:00,590 --> 00:02:04,120
And for the forest on its banks, the showed only ranks of skeletons.

26
00:02:05,690 --> 00:02:07,079
April advanced to May.

27
00:02:07,989 --> 00:02:12,867
A bright serene May it was, days of blue sky, placid sunshine,

28
00:02:12,868 --> 00:02:16,120
and soft western or southern gales filled up its duration.

29
00:02:16,829 --> 00:02:19,440
And now, vegetation matured with vigor.

30
00:02:20,219 --> 00:02:22,000
Lowwood shook loose its tresses.

31
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:24,420
It became all green, all flowery.

32
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:30,020
Its great elm ash and oak skeletons were restored to majestic life.

33
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:34,600
Woodland plants sprang up profusely in its recesses.

34
00:02:35,559 --> 00:02:40,559
Unnumbered varieties of moss filled its hollows, and it made a strange ground

35
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:43,819
sunshine out of the wealth of its wild primrose plants.

36
00:02:44,690 --> 00:02:48,144
I have seen their pale gold gleam in overshadowed

37
00:02:48,145 --> 00:02:51,299
spots like scattering of the sweetest luster.

38
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,319
All this I enjoyed often and fully, free, unwatched and almost alone.

39
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:02,328
For this unwanted liberty and pleasure, there was

40
00:03:02,329 --> 00:03:05,920
a cause, to which it now becomes my task to advert.

41
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:12,399
Have I not described a pleasant sight for a dwelling, when I speak of it as busomed

42
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:15,680
in hill and wood, and rising from the verge of a stream?

43
00:03:17,579 --> 00:03:18,819
Assuredly pleasant enough.

44
00:03:19,819 --> 00:03:22,500
But whether healthy or not is another question.

45
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:28,479
That forest del, where lowered lay, was the cradle of fog and fog-bred

46
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:30,962
pestilence, which, quickening with the

47
00:03:30,963 --> 00:03:35,001
quickening spring, crept into the orphan asylum.

48
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:40,420
Breathe typhus through its crowded schoolroom and dormitory, and air may

49
00:03:40,421 --> 00:03:44,399
arrived, transformed the seminary into a hospital.

50
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:49,859
Semi-starvation and neglected colds had predisposed

51
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:52,841
most of the pupils to receive infection.

52
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:57,959
45 out of the 80 girls lay ill at one time.

53
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:02,219
Classes were broken up, rules relaxed.

54
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:08,940
The few who continued well, were allowed almost unlimited license, because the

55
00:04:08,941 --> 00:04:11,472
medical attendant insisted on the necessity

56
00:04:11,474 --> 00:04:14,181
of frequent exercise to keep them in health.

57
00:04:15,290 --> 00:04:20,340
And had it been otherwise, no one had leisure to watch or restrain them.

58
00:04:21,290 --> 00:04:24,579
Miss Temple's whole attention was absorbed by the patience.

59
00:04:25,610 --> 00:04:28,805
She lived in the sick room, never quitting it,

60
00:04:28,806 --> 00:04:32,681
except to snatch a few hours rest at night.

61
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:38,059
The teachers were fully occupied with packing up and making other necessary

62
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:43,559
preparations for the departure of those girls who were fortunate enough to have

63
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:48,420
friends and relations able and willing to remove them from the seat of contagion.

64
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:53,559
Many, already smitten, went home only to die.

65
00:04:54,670 --> 00:04:58,086
Some died at the school, and were buried quietly and

66
00:04:58,087 --> 00:05:01,799
quickly, the nature of the malady forbidding delay.

67
00:05:02,790 --> 00:05:08,059
While disease had thus become an inhabitant of lowwood, and death its

68
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:12,700
frequent visitor, while there was gloom and fear within its walls.

69
00:05:13,690 --> 00:05:19,399
While its rooms and passages steamed with hospital smells, the drug and the pastils

70
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:24,899
striving vainly to overcome the affluvia of mortality, that bright may shone

71
00:05:24,900 --> 00:05:29,280
unclouded over the bold hills and beautiful woodland out of doors.

72
00:05:30,459 --> 00:05:32,780
It's garden too glowed with flowers.

73
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:37,555
Hollyhocks had sprung up tall as trees, lilies

74
00:05:37,556 --> 00:05:41,261
had opened, tulips and roses were in bloom.

75
00:05:41,809 --> 00:05:46,980
The borders of the little beds were gay with pink thrift and crimson double -daisies.

76
00:05:47,250 --> 00:05:52,319
The sweet briars gave out, mourning and evening, their scent of spice and apples,

77
00:05:53,149 --> 00:05:58,521
and these fragrant treasures were all useless for most of the inmates of lowwood.

78
00:05:58,780 --> 00:06:04,261
Except to furnish now and then, a handful of herbs and blossoms to put in a coffin.

79
00:06:05,350 --> 00:06:08,986
But I, and the rest who continued well, enjoyed

80
00:06:08,987 --> 00:06:12,460
fully the beauties of the scene and season.

81
00:06:13,379 --> 00:06:18,059
They let us ramble in the wood, like gypsies, from morning till night.

82
00:06:19,100 --> 00:06:21,920
We did what we liked, went where we liked.

83
00:06:22,819 --> 00:06:24,079
We lived better too.

84
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:29,260
Mr. Brockelhurst and his family never came near lowwood now.

85
00:06:30,399 --> 00:06:33,379
Household matters were not scrutinized into.

86
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:38,840
The crosshousekeeper was gone, driven away by the fear of infection.

87
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:46,020
Her successor, who had been matron at the Lauten dispensary, unused to the ways of

88
00:06:46,021 --> 00:06:50,379
her new abode, provided with comparative liberality.

89
00:06:51,299 --> 00:06:53,940
Besides, there were fewer to feed.

90
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:56,299
The sick could eat little.

91
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,965
Our breakfast basins were better filled when there was no

92
00:07:00,966 --> 00:07:04,440
time to prepare a regular dinner, which often happened.

93
00:07:05,510 --> 00:07:10,760
She would give us a large piece of cold pie, or a thick slice of bread and cheese,

94
00:07:11,659 --> 00:07:14,790
and this we carried away with us to the wood, where we

95
00:07:14,790 --> 00:07:19,500
each chose the spot we liked best and dined sumptuously.

96
00:07:20,579 --> 00:07:26,819
My favorite seat was a smooth and broad stone, rising white and dry from the very

97
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:31,139
middle of the back, and only to be got at by wading through the water.

98
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,219
A feet, I accomplished barefoot.

99
00:07:35,209 --> 00:07:42,040
The stone was just broad enough to accommodate comfortably another girl in me.

100
00:07:42,329 --> 00:07:47,920
At that time my chosen comrade, one Marianne Wilson, a shrewd,

101
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:53,219
observant personage whose society I took pleasure in, partly because she was witty

102
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:57,520
and original, and partly because she had a manor which set me at my ease.

103
00:07:58,430 --> 00:08:01,966
Some years older than I, she knew more of the world,

104
00:08:01,967 --> 00:08:05,101
and could tell me many things I liked to hear.

105
00:08:05,969 --> 00:08:08,719
With her, my curiosity found gratification.

106
00:08:09,770 --> 00:08:13,457
To my faults also she gave ample indulgence,

107
00:08:13,458 --> 00:08:16,800
never imposing curb or rain on anything I said.

108
00:08:17,790 --> 00:08:22,739
She had a turn for narrative, eye for analysis, she liked to inform eye to

109
00:08:22,740 --> 00:08:27,520
question, so we got on swimmingly together, deriving much entertainment,

110
00:08:28,059 --> 00:08:31,219
if not much improvement, from our mutual intercourse.

111
00:08:32,350 --> 00:08:34,700
And where meantime was Helen Burns?

112
00:08:36,379 --> 00:08:39,620
Why did I not spend these sweet days of liberty with her?

113
00:08:40,319 --> 00:08:41,420
Had I forgotten her?

114
00:08:42,370 --> 00:08:46,799
Or was I so worthless as to have grown tired of her pure society?

115
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:53,521
Surely the Marianne Wilson I have mentioned was inferior to my first acquaintance.

116
00:08:54,194 --> 00:08:57,837
She could only tell me amusing stories and reciprocate

117
00:08:57,838 --> 00:09:01,200
any racy and pungent gossip I chose to indulge in.

118
00:09:01,989 --> 00:09:07,799
While, if I have spoken truth of Helen, she was qualified to give those who

119
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:12,079
enjoyed the privilege of her converse, a taste of far higher things.

120
00:09:13,569 --> 00:09:16,600
True reader, and I knew and felt this.

121
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:22,500
And though I am a defective being, with many faults and few redeeming points,

122
00:09:23,530 --> 00:09:25,819
yet I never tired of Helen Burns.

123
00:09:26,959 --> 00:09:31,860
Nor ever ceased to cherish for her a sentiment of attachment, a strong,

124
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:36,719
tender, and respectful, as any that ever animated my heart.

125
00:09:38,219 --> 00:09:43,700
How could it be otherwise when Helen, at all times and under all circumstances,

126
00:09:44,659 --> 00:09:50,420
evinced for me a quiet and faithful friendship which ill-human never soured,

127
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:52,860
nor irritation never troubled?

128
00:09:53,879 --> 00:09:55,860
But Helen was ill at present.

129
00:09:56,659 --> 00:10:02,161
For some weeks she had been removed from my sight, to I knew not what room upstairs.

130
00:10:02,409 --> 00:10:04,769
She was not, I was told, in the hospital

131
00:10:04,770 --> 00:10:08,140
portion of the house with the fever patients.

132
00:10:08,510 --> 00:10:11,819
For her complaint was consumption, not typhus.

133
00:10:12,409 --> 00:10:16,921
And by consumption, I, in my ignorance, understood something

134
00:10:16,922 --> 00:10:20,399
mild, which time and care would be sure to alleviate.

135
00:10:21,879 --> 00:10:27,379
I was confirmed in this idea by the fact of her once or twice, coming downstairs on

136
00:10:27,380 --> 00:10:33,040
very warm, sunny afternoons, and being taken by Miss Temple into the garden.

137
00:10:34,290 --> 00:10:39,059
But, on these occasions, I was not allowed to go and speak to her.

138
00:10:39,799 --> 00:10:44,440
I only saw her from the school-room window, and then, not distinctly,

139
00:10:45,110 --> 00:10:49,780
for she was much wrapped up and sat at a distance under the veranda.

140
00:10:51,010 --> 00:10:53,808
One evening, in the beginning of June, I had

141
00:10:53,808 --> 00:10:57,101
stayed out very late with Marianne in the wood.

142
00:10:57,280 --> 00:11:03,180
We had, as usual, separated ourselves from the others, and had wandered far,

143
00:11:03,840 --> 00:11:09,920
so far that we lost our way, and had to ask it at a lonely cottage, where a man

144
00:11:09,921 --> 00:11:12,815
and woman lived, who looked after a herd of

145
00:11:12,816 --> 00:11:16,700
half-wild swine that fed on the mast in the wood.

146
00:11:17,420 --> 00:11:24,200
When we got back, it was after Moonrise, a pony, which we knew to be the surgeons,

147
00:11:24,899 --> 00:11:26,780
was standing at the garden door.

148
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:33,233
Marianne remarked that she supposed someone must be very ill, as Mr.

149
00:11:33,234 --> 00:11:35,520
Bates had been sent for at that time of the evening.

150
00:11:37,190 --> 00:11:38,200
She went into the house.

151
00:11:39,225 --> 00:11:44,040
I stayed behind a few minutes to plant in my garden a handful of roots I had dug up

152
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:48,159
in the forest, and which I feared would wither if I left them till the morning.

153
00:11:49,799 --> 00:11:52,799
This done, I lingered yet a little longer.

154
00:11:53,659 --> 00:11:56,520
The flowers smelled so sweet as the dew fell.

155
00:11:57,340 --> 00:12:00,840
It was such a pleasant evening, so serene, so warm.

156
00:12:01,610 --> 00:12:05,979
The still glowing west promised so fairly another fine day on the morrow,

157
00:12:06,739 --> 00:12:09,920
the moon rose with such majesty in the grave east.

158
00:12:10,909 --> 00:12:14,112
I was noting these things and enjoying them as a child

159
00:12:14,113 --> 00:12:17,840
might, when it entered my mind as it had never done before.

160
00:12:19,399 --> 00:12:24,340
How sad to be lying now on a sick bed, and to be in danger of dying.

161
00:12:25,219 --> 00:12:26,360
This world is pleasant.

162
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:30,979
It would be dreary to be called from it and to have to go who knows where.

163
00:12:32,649 --> 00:12:38,360
And then my mind made its first earnest effort to comprehend what had been infused

164
00:12:38,361 --> 00:12:43,340
into it concerning heaven and hell, and for the first time it recoiled,

165
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:49,139
baffled, and for the first time glancing behind on each side and before it,

166
00:12:49,799 --> 00:12:52,700
it saw all round an unfathomned gulf.

167
00:12:53,500 --> 00:12:57,860
It felt the one point where it stood, the present, all the rest was formless

168
00:12:57,861 --> 00:13:01,627
cloud and vacant depth, and it shuddered at the

169
00:13:01,628 --> 00:13:05,120
thought of tottering and plunging amid that chaos.

170
00:13:06,590 --> 00:13:11,468
While pondering this new idea, I heard the front door open, Mr.

171
00:13:11,468 --> 00:13:13,799
Bates came out and with him was a nurse.

172
00:13:15,420 --> 00:13:19,739
After she had seen him mount his horse and depart, she was about to close the door,

173
00:13:20,370 --> 00:13:21,370
but I ran up to her.

174
00:13:22,260 --> 00:13:23,260
How was Helen Burns?

175
00:13:24,969 --> 00:13:26,979
Very poorly was the answer.

176
00:13:28,750 --> 00:13:30,940
Is it her Mr. Bates has been to see?

177
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:34,799
Yes, and what does he say about her?

178
00:13:35,879 --> 00:13:38,280
He says she'll not be here long.

179
00:13:39,719 --> 00:13:44,459
This phrase, uttered in my hearing yesterday, would have only conveyed the

180
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:48,840
notion that she was about to be removed to Northumberland, to her own home.

181
00:13:50,180 --> 00:13:57,020
I should not have suspected that it meant she was dying, but I knew instantly now.

182
00:13:58,420 --> 00:14:04,500
It opened clear on my comprehension that Helen Burns was numbering her last days in

183
00:14:04,501 --> 00:14:08,260
this world, and that she was going to be taken to the region of spirits,

184
00:14:09,045 --> 00:14:10,180
if such region there were.

185
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:16,540
I experienced a shock of horror, then a strong thrill of grief,

186
00:14:17,209 --> 00:14:22,959
then a desire, a necessity to see her, and I asked in what room she lay.

187
00:14:24,514 --> 00:14:26,979
She is in Miss Temple's room, said the nurse.

188
00:14:28,049 --> 00:14:29,500
May I go up and speak to her?

189
00:14:30,489 --> 00:14:31,639
Oh no, child.

190
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:35,680
It is not likely, and now it is time for you to come in.

191
00:14:36,459 --> 00:14:39,020
You'll catch the fever if you stop out when the dew is falling.

192
00:14:41,424 --> 00:14:43,120
The nurse closed the front door.

193
00:14:43,739 --> 00:14:46,700
I went in by the side entrance which led to the school room.

194
00:14:47,719 --> 00:14:49,200
I was just in time.

195
00:14:50,239 --> 00:14:53,899
It was 9 o'clock, and Miss Miller was calling the pupils to go to bed.

196
00:14:55,139 --> 00:15:01,920
It might be two hours later, probably near 11, when I, not having been able to fall

197
00:15:01,921 --> 00:15:07,159
asleep and deeming from the perfect silence of the dormitory, that my

198
00:15:07,161 --> 00:15:14,040
companions were all wrapped in profound repose, rose softly, put on my frock over

199
00:15:14,040 --> 00:15:18,212
my nitrous, and, without shoes, crept from the

200
00:15:18,213 --> 00:15:21,600
apartment, and set off in quest of Miss Temple's room.

201
00:15:23,319 --> 00:15:28,979
It was quite at the other end of the house, but I knew my way, and the light of

202
00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:32,748
the unclouded summer moon entering here and there at

203
00:15:32,750 --> 00:15:35,979
passage windows enabled me to find it without difficulty.

204
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:42,040
An odor of camphor and burnt vinegar warned me when I came near the fever room,

205
00:15:42,899 --> 00:15:45,742
and I passed its door quickly, fearful less

206
00:15:45,743 --> 00:15:48,280
the nurse who sat up all night should hear me.

207
00:15:49,559 --> 00:15:54,120
I dreaded being discovered and sent back, for I must see Helen.

208
00:15:54,909 --> 00:15:56,639
I must embrace her before she died.

209
00:15:57,850 --> 00:16:02,860
I must give her one last kiss, exchange with her one last word.

210
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:07,426
Having descended a staircase, traversed a portion of the house

211
00:16:07,427 --> 00:16:11,200
below, and succeeded in opening and shutting, without noise.

212
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:17,024
Two doors I reached another flight of steps, these I mounted,

213
00:16:17,025 --> 00:16:20,920
and then just opposite to me, was Miss Temple's room.

214
00:16:22,049 --> 00:16:25,746
A light shone through the keyhole and from under the

215
00:16:25,747 --> 00:16:28,739
door, a profound stillness pervaded the vicinity.

216
00:16:30,069 --> 00:16:35,860
Coming near, I found the door slightly ajar, probably to admit some fresh air

217
00:16:36,059 --> 00:16:37,680
into the close abode of sickness.

218
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:44,219
Indisposed to hesitate, and full of impatient impulses, soul and senses

219
00:16:44,220 --> 00:16:48,280
quivering with keen throes, I put it back and looked in.

220
00:16:49,370 --> 00:16:52,500
My eye sought Helen, and feared to find death.

221
00:16:53,879 --> 00:16:57,879
Close by Miss Temple's bed, and half covered with its white curtains,

222
00:16:58,579 --> 00:16:59,700
there stood a little crib.

223
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:06,900
I saw the outline of a form under the clothes, but the face was hid by the hangings.

224
00:17:07,260 --> 00:17:11,522
The nurse I had spoken to in the garden sat in an easy

225
00:17:11,522 --> 00:17:15,940
chair asleep, an unsnuffed candle burnt dimly on the table.

226
00:17:16,959 --> 00:17:19,319
Miss Temple was not to be seen.

227
00:17:20,180 --> 00:17:24,559
I knew afterwards that she had been called to a delirious patient in the fever room.

228
00:17:25,719 --> 00:17:28,319
I advanced, then paused by the cribside.

229
00:17:29,180 --> 00:17:34,579
My hand was on the curtain, but I preferred speaking before I withdrew it.

230
00:17:35,340 --> 00:17:38,000
I still recoiled at the dread of seeing a corpse.

231
00:17:39,559 --> 00:17:40,559
Helen?

232
00:17:40,780 --> 00:17:41,780
I whispered softly.

233
00:17:42,219 --> 00:17:43,219
Are you awake?

234
00:17:43,790 --> 00:17:48,879
She stirred herself, put back the curtain, and I saw her face, pale, wasted,

235
00:17:49,420 --> 00:17:50,420
but quite composed.

236
00:17:51,135 --> 00:17:55,200
She looked so little-changed that my fear was instantly dissipated.

237
00:17:56,649 --> 00:17:57,649
Can it be you, Jane?

238
00:17:58,409 --> 00:18:00,879
She asked in her own gentle voice.

239
00:18:02,104 --> 00:18:05,520
Oh, I thought, she's not going to die.

240
00:18:06,079 --> 00:18:07,079
They are mistaken.

241
00:18:07,559 --> 00:18:09,860
She cannot speak and look so calmly if she were.

242
00:18:11,229 --> 00:18:12,860
I got on to her crib and kissed her.

243
00:18:13,760 --> 00:18:16,994
Her forehead was cold, and her cheek both cold and thin,

244
00:18:16,995 --> 00:18:21,700
and so were her hand and wrist, but she smiled as of old.

245
00:18:23,530 --> 00:18:24,819
Why are you come here, Jane?

246
00:18:25,700 --> 00:18:26,860
It has passed eleven o'clock.

247
00:18:27,670 --> 00:18:29,520
I heard it strike some minutes since.

248
00:18:31,620 --> 00:18:32,840
I came to see you, Helen.

249
00:18:33,729 --> 00:18:38,000
I heard you were very ill, and I could not sleep till I had spoken to you.

250
00:18:38,930 --> 00:18:40,250
You came to bid me goodbye, then.

251
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:42,639
You were just in time, probably.

252
00:18:43,700 --> 00:18:44,940
Are you going somewhere, Helen?

253
00:18:45,350 --> 00:18:46,350
Are you going home?

254
00:18:47,100 --> 00:18:48,100
Yes.

255
00:18:48,180 --> 00:18:50,760
To my long home, my last home.

256
00:18:51,399 --> 00:18:52,579
No, no, Helen.

257
00:18:53,610 --> 00:18:54,899
I stopped distressed.

258
00:18:55,840 --> 00:19:00,440
While I tried to devour my tears, a fit of coughing seized Helen.

259
00:19:01,079 --> 00:19:02,659
It did not, however, wake the nurse.

260
00:19:03,360 --> 00:19:07,659
When it was over, she lay some minutes exhausted, then she whispered.

261
00:19:09,159 --> 00:19:11,559
Jane, your little feet are bare.

262
00:19:12,280 --> 00:19:14,459
Lie down and cover yourself with my quilt.

263
00:19:15,399 --> 00:19:16,399
I did so.

264
00:19:16,700 --> 00:19:19,979
She put her arm over me, and I nestled close to her.

265
00:19:20,719 --> 00:19:24,100
After a long silence, she resumed, still whispering.

266
00:19:25,340 --> 00:19:28,144
I am very happy, Jane, and when you hear that

267
00:19:28,145 --> 00:19:31,681
I am dead, you must be sure and not grieve.

268
00:19:32,129 --> 00:19:33,620
There is nothing to grieve about.

269
00:19:35,120 --> 00:19:40,540
We almost die one day, and the illness which is removing me is not painful.

270
00:19:41,299 --> 00:19:42,940
It is gentle and gradual.

271
00:19:43,569 --> 00:19:44,840
My mind is at rest.

272
00:19:45,719 --> 00:19:47,920
I leave no one to regret me much.

273
00:19:48,639 --> 00:19:52,739
I have only a father, and he is lately married and will not miss me.

274
00:19:53,280 --> 00:19:56,799
By dying young, I shall escape great sufferings.

275
00:19:57,670 --> 00:20:01,520
I had not qualities or talents to make my way very well in the world.

276
00:20:02,319 --> 00:20:04,239
I should have been continually at fault.

277
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:07,139
But where are you going to, Helen?

278
00:20:07,440 --> 00:20:07,860
Can you see?

279
00:20:08,280 --> 00:20:09,280
Do you know?

280
00:20:09,500 --> 00:20:12,540
I believe I have faith I am going to God.

281
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:14,100
Where is God?

282
00:20:14,299 --> 00:20:15,299
What is God?

283
00:20:16,299 --> 00:20:20,360
My maker and yours, who will never destroy what he created.

284
00:20:21,139 --> 00:20:25,559
I rely implicitly on his power and confide wholly in his goodness.

285
00:20:26,500 --> 00:20:30,760
I count the hours till that eventful one arrives, which shall restore me to him.

286
00:20:31,459 --> 00:20:32,899
Reveal him to me.

287
00:20:34,909 --> 00:20:38,966
You are sure then, Helen, that there is such a place as

288
00:20:38,967 --> 00:20:42,139
heaven, and that our souls can get to it when we die.

289
00:20:43,590 --> 00:20:45,600
I am sure there is a future state.

290
00:20:46,340 --> 00:20:47,420
I believe God is good.

291
00:20:48,200 --> 00:20:51,139
I can resign my immortal part to him without any misgiving.

292
00:20:52,180 --> 00:20:53,200
God is my father.

293
00:20:54,040 --> 00:20:55,040
God is my friend.

294
00:20:55,799 --> 00:20:56,799
I love him.

295
00:20:57,299 --> 00:20:58,600
I believe he loves me.

296
00:21:00,170 --> 00:21:02,059
And shall I see you again, Helen, when I die?

297
00:21:03,780 --> 00:21:06,260
You will come to the same region of happiness.

298
00:21:07,040 --> 00:21:09,959
Be received by the same mighty universal parent.

299
00:21:10,420 --> 00:21:11,440
No doubt, dear Jane.

300
00:21:12,559 --> 00:21:15,440
Again, I questioned, but this time only in thought.

301
00:21:16,700 --> 00:21:17,700
Where is that region?

302
00:21:18,500 --> 00:21:19,500
Does it exist?

303
00:21:20,260 --> 00:21:22,680
And I clasped my arms closer around Helen.

304
00:21:23,559 --> 00:21:25,299
She seemed dearer to me than ever.

305
00:21:26,139 --> 00:21:27,620
I felt as if I could not let her go.

306
00:21:28,489 --> 00:21:30,520
I lay with my face hidden on her neck.

307
00:21:31,364 --> 00:21:36,780
Presently, she said in the sweetest tone, how comfortable I am.

308
00:21:37,389 --> 00:21:40,000
That last fit of coffin has tired me a little.

309
00:21:40,799 --> 00:21:44,079
I feel as if I could sleep, but don't leave me, Jane.

310
00:21:44,899 --> 00:21:46,239
I like to have you near me.

311
00:21:47,579 --> 00:21:48,979
I'll stay with you, dear Helen.

312
00:21:49,500 --> 00:21:50,739
No one shall take me away.

313
00:21:52,250 --> 00:21:53,250
Are you warm, darling?

314
00:21:54,100 --> 00:21:55,100
Yes.

315
00:21:55,420 --> 00:21:56,420
Good night, Jane.

316
00:21:57,139 --> 00:21:58,139
Good night, Helen.

317
00:21:58,860 --> 00:22:02,899
She kissed me and I her, and we both soon slumbered.

318
00:22:03,959 --> 00:22:07,319
When I awoke it was day, and unusual movement roused me.

319
00:22:07,579 --> 00:22:08,579
I looked up.

320
00:22:08,659 --> 00:22:09,760
I was in somebody's arms.

321
00:22:10,280 --> 00:22:11,280
The nurse held me.

322
00:22:11,819 --> 00:22:14,799
She was carrying me through the passage back to the dormitory.

323
00:22:15,780 --> 00:22:17,879
I was not reprimanded for leaving my bed.

324
00:22:18,885 --> 00:22:20,579
People had something else to think about.

325
00:22:21,299 --> 00:22:24,440
No explanation was afforded then to my many questions.

326
00:22:25,420 --> 00:22:30,500
But a day or two afterwards, I learned that Miss Temple, on returning to her own

327
00:22:30,501 --> 00:22:34,444
room at dawn, had found me laid in the little crib, my face

328
00:22:34,445 --> 00:22:38,239
against Helen Burns's shoulder, my arms around her neck.

329
00:22:39,329 --> 00:22:42,659
I was asleep, and Helen was dead.

330
00:22:45,110 --> 00:22:46,879
Her grave is in Brockobridge Churchyard.

331
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:53,340
For 15 years after her death, it was only covered by a grassy mound.

332
00:22:54,360 --> 00:23:00,540
But now, a gray marble tablet marks the spot, inscribed with her name,

333
00:23:01,200 --> 00:23:02,819
and the word Rasurgam.
